This distribution of the "jip toolset" consists of 5 coordinated programs:
1) jip          command-oriented "scripting" tool
2) jip-display  4-D display package
3) jip-align    registration using affine + non-linear warps
4) jip-glm      fMRI analyis using the General Linear Model (1st level)
5) jip-rem      fMRI analyis using a random-effects model (2nd level GLM)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Execution:  *** ALTER define-jip.tcsh ***
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Edit "define_jip.tcsh" on this directory to define the disk location and aliases
for the 5 programs above.

You may want to put this into your shell startup file (e.g., .cshrc for csh).
     source wherever/define_jip.tcsh 1
where "wherever/" is the root location of jip tools.  The argument ("1", or anything else)
suppresses text output.

Note that each alias in "define_jip.tcsh" points to an ASCII file, which picks
the correct executable (if it exists) for the current machine type (e.g., Linux-i686)
using `uname -s`-`uname -m`. The exception is for the Mac OS X (Darwin) executable,
which is a universal binary containing ppc, 32-bit (i386), and 64-bit (x86_64) executables.

These executables have been provided directly without need for compilation.
Note that the Darwin (Mac OS X) executable reports "i686" to "uname -m", but
the architecture of this executable is 64-bit (x86_64), as reported by the "file" command.

uname -s	uname -m	architecture
--------	--------	------------
Linux		i686		32 bit
Linux		x86_64		64 bit
CYGWIN_NT-5.1	i686		32 bit (Cygwin/X for Windows)
Darwin				ppc, 32-bit 64 bit

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Documentation:
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
This (such as it is) resides on directory $JIP_HOME/documentation.
Documentation is designed to be used from the programs.  E.g.,
"xd -h" reads the file "display-startup.txt" on $JIP_HOME/documentation.
"xd -H" reads the file "display-menu.txt"    on $JIP_HOME/documentation.
Documentation modes:
1) startup: "xd -h", "align -h", "glm -h"
2) menu   : "xd -H", "align -H", "glm -H", "jip" followed by "help"
3) "h" button in the display and alignment GUIs
4) "?" button in the display and alignment GUIs

Check the documentation first before asking questions. I'll try to add to
the documentation as I respond to questions.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compilation (when a new executable is needed):
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
To facilitate compilation, I have created a directory named "build-jip".
On this directory, try using "build-all.tcsh". As the extension indicates,
scripts in this directory using the tcsh shell. If you are using another shell
like bash, you will need to switch to tcsh to execute these scripts:
unix% tcsh
unix% source build-all.tcsh

Other scripts on this directory can be used to compile separate parts.

Alternatively,go to each of the 5 directories corresponding to the tools above:
1) $JIP_HOME/jip
2) $JIP_HOME/jip-display
3) $JIP_HOME/jip-align
4) $JIP_HOME/jip-glm
5) $JIP_HOME/jip-rem

On each directory, follow instructions in the file REAME.jip

REQUIRED LIBRARIES
The libraries below are required PRIOR to compiling each executable above.
Create the libraries in the order below.

1) Data compression library zlib (needed for znz libary, which is used by NIFTILIB)
   Go to $JIP_HOME/zlib-1.2.5 and read the file README.jip
2) NIFTI i/o library and znz library
   Go to $JIP_HOME/nifticlib-2.2.0 and read the file README.jip
3) FFTW (Fastest Fourier Transform in the West)
   go to $JIP_HOME/fftw-3.2.2 and read the file README.jip
   This library takes a long time to create.
4) jip-specific libraries:
   Go to $JIP_HOME/lib and read the file README.jip
5) X11 library. This should reside somewhere on your system.
   Common places include /usr/lib, /usr/local/lib, /usr/X11R6/lib
6) Standard system libraries from /usr/lib


